# Audit results and detailed explanations, part A (questions q1-q120).
# Each entry: question id, the start of its stem (a safety check that the id still points at the same question),
# and a fuller explanation of why the right answer is correct. Optional fixes: opts=[correct first, 3 wrong]
# replaces the answer choices; q= replaces the stem. Audited Nov 2026.
E = {}
def e(qid, stem, exp, opts=None, q=None):
    E[qid] = dict(stem=stem, exp=exp, opts=opts, q=q)

e("q1", "A business owner wants", "The balance sheet answers 'what do we own and owe right now?' It lists assets, liabilities and owner's equity as of one specific date, such as December 31, and always balances: assets = liabilities + equity. The income statement and cash flow statement cover a span of time, like a year, and the sales budget is a plan, not a record.")
e("q2", "Revenue minus cost of", "Gross profit is what's left after subtracting the direct cost of the products sold (COGS) from sales revenue. It shows how profitable the products themselves are before overhead. Net income comes later, after operating expenses, interest and taxes are also subtracted; retained earnings is an equity account, not an income statement line.")
e("q3", "Which of the following would appear as a cash inflow", "Selling the van brings cash into the business, so it's an inflow, reported under investing activities because a long-term asset was sold. Paying suppliers, paying wages and repaying a loan all send cash out, so they're outflows (operating, operating and financing).")
e("q4", "A company buys new ovens", "Investing activities are purchases and sales of long-term assets such as equipment, buildings and investments. Ovens will be used for years, so buying them is an investing outflow. Operating activities are day-to-day items like sales and wages; financing activities involve lenders and owners.")
e("q5", "Thornbury Tools paid", "Financing activities are cash flows with the company's owners and lenders: issuing stock, paying dividends, borrowing and repaying loans. A dividend sends cash to shareholders, so it's a financing outflow. It isn't operating because paying owners isn't part of producing or selling the product.")
e("q6", "Net income earned and kept", "Retained earnings is the running total of profits the company has kept instead of paying out as dividends. Each year net income is added and dividends are subtracted. Contributed capital is money owners invested by buying stock, and payables are liabilities, not equity.")
e("q7", "A corporation pays its shareholders", "A dividend is a distribution of profit to the owners, not a cost of running the business, so it never appears as an expense. On the statement of shareholders' equity it's shown in the dividends line and reduces retained earnings, which lowers total equity.")
e("q8", "After comparing what she forecast", "A variance is the gap between a planned (budgeted or forecast) figure and the actual result. Sales coming in 12% below forecast is an unfavorable sales variance. Spotting it lets the owner ask why, whether it's slow traffic, pricing or a competitor, and adjust.")
e("q9", "Which is the most important reason", "Accurate financial information lets an owner make informed decisions: what to charge, when to hire, whether to borrow and where to cut costs. The other choices are impossible promises; no information can guarantee profits, eliminate taxes or replace planning.")
e("q10", "The finance function of a business", "Finance gets money and puts it to its best use. That includes managing working capital (cash, receivables, inventory, payables) and capital budgeting, deciding which long-term investments, like new equipment, are worth making. Advertising belongs to marketing, recruiting to HR and quality standards to operations.")
e("q11", "Assets = $250,000", "The accounting equation is assets = liabilities + owner's equity. Rearranged, equity = assets - liabilities = $250,000 - $90,000 = $160,000. Equity is the owners' claim on the assets after creditors are paid.")
e("q12", "Which item would NOT appear on an income", "The income statement reports revenues and expenses over a period. Sales revenue, rent expense and cost of goods sold all belong there. Cash on hand is an asset, something the business owns at a point in time, so it appears on the balance sheet instead.")
e("q13", "Under accrual accounting", "Accrual accounting follows the revenue recognition principle: revenue is recorded when it's earned, meaning the work is done, not when cash arrives. The project was finished in March, so March gets the revenue; April's payment just turns the receivable into cash.")
e("q14", "A growing manufacturer wants to raise", "Selling stock to the public for the first time is an initial public offering, and investment banks run IPOs. They underwrite the shares, set the price, file with the SEC and find investors. Credit unions and savings institutions take deposits and make loans, and insurers manage risk.")
e("q15", "Which financial institution is a not-for-profit", "A credit union is a cooperative: its members are its owners, and it operates not-for-profit, returning earnings as lower loan rates, higher savings rates and fewer fees. Commercial banks, investment banks and brokerages are owned by shareholders and run for profit.")
e("q16", "Which international institution provides", "The World Bank Group lends money and gives technical advice to governments of developing countries for projects such as schools, roads and clean water, with the goal of reducing poverty. The Federal Reserve and FDIC are U.S. agencies, and the NYSE is a stock exchange.")
e("q17", "The primary purpose of an insurance", "Insurance companies exist to transfer and pool risk. Customers pay a predictable premium, and the insurer pays for covered losses that would be too large to handle alone. That minimizes the customer's financial risk; growing investments and getting mortgages are other institutions' jobs.")
e("q18", "Treasury bills that mature", "The money market is where short-term debt, maturing in one year or less, is bought and sold. A 13-week Treasury bill is short-term, very safe and highly liquid, the classic money market instrument. The capital market handles long-term stocks and bonds.")
e("q19", "Investors who buy and sell shares", "In an IPO, the company sells new shares in the primary market and receives the money. After that, investors trade those shares with each other on exchanges like the NYSE or Nasdaq; that's the secondary market, and the company doesn't receive money from those trades.")
e("q20", "A trader buys oil futures", "Oil is a commodity, a raw material traded in bulk, and a futures contract locks in a price for delivery later. Futures on oil, metals and grains trade in the commodities market, for example on the CME. Producers and airlines use them to hedge price swings.")
e("q21", "Contracts such as options and swaps", "A derivative is a contract whose value comes from something else, such as a stock price, interest rate, currency or commodity. Options, futures and swaps are all derivatives and trade in the derivatives market. They're used to hedge risk or to speculate.")
e("q22", "A national bank merges with a large insurance", "Convergence is when firms from different financial sectors, like banking and insurance, combine or offer each other's products, creating one-stop shopping. Consolidation would be two firms from the same sector merging, such as two banks.")
e("q23", "Two regional banks merge", "When two firms in the same industry combine, the result is consolidation: fewer, larger companies. Banks consolidate for economies of scale, a wider branch network and more capital. Convergence and bancassurance involve combining different types of financial services.")
e("q24", "A strategy in which a bank sells insurance", "Bancassurance (bank + assurance) is when a bank sells insurance policies through its branches and to its existing customers. It's a convergence strategy that earns the bank fee income and gives the insurer a ready sales channel.")
e("q25", "During an economic contraction", "In a contraction, GDP, employment and consumer spending fall. Lower sales mean lower expected company earnings, and since stock prices reflect expected earnings, prices in most industries decline. Defensive sectors like groceries and health care usually fall less.")
e("q26", "When a country's unemployment", "When many people are out of work, they produce nothing and earn less, so they spend less. Lower spending means businesses sell and produce less, and the economy's total output (GDP) falls. High unemployment usually comes with weak spending, not rising spending or fast inflation.")
e("q27", "Shares of utility and grocery", "Utilities and groceries are defensive industries: people keep paying electric bills and buying food no matter how the economy is doing. Steady demand means steady earnings, so their stocks tend to hold value better in recessions than cyclical stocks like travel or luxury goods.")
e("q28", "An investor keeps nearly all", "Home bias is the tendency to invest mostly in your own country's market because it feels familiar and safer. It sacrifices diversification, since foreign markets don't always move with U.S. markets. Herd mentality means following the crowd, a different bias.")
e("q29", "Which is a common characteristic of emerging", "Emerging markets are economies that are industrializing and growing quickly, such as India or Brazil. Their growth can produce big gains, but political change, currency swings and less mature markets also cause sharp drops, so volatility is their defining feature.")
e("q30", "A country holds an unexpected election", "Investors move money toward stability. Political uncertainty raises the risk of policy changes, so foreign investors may pull money out and sell the currency. More sellers than buyers pushes the currency's value down, at least until the outcome is clear.")
e("q31", "A public company announces that its CEO", "Form 8-K is the SEC's 'current report' for major unscheduled events, such as an executive leaving, a merger or a bankruptcy, and is generally due within four business days. The 10-K is the annual report; W-2 and 1040 are tax forms.")
e("q32", "Which source contains a corporation's audited", "A public company's annual report, and the 10-K it files with the SEC, contains audited financial statements plus management's discussion and analysis of the year's results, risks and outlook. A stock table shows only trading data, and a credit report covers borrowing history.")
e("q33", "In a stock table, a blank in the dividend", "The dividend column shows the annual dividend per share. A blank means the company isn't currently paying one, which is common for growth companies that reinvest profits. It doesn't mean the company is bankrupt or unlisted.")
e("q34", "Which stock table column helps", "The 52-week high and low show the highest and lowest prices over the past year. A wide range means the price has swung a lot, which signals volatility; a narrow range means it's been stable. Ticker, volume and dividend don't show price swings over time.")
e("q35", "A stock's relative strength line", "A relative strength line compares a stock's price performance with a market index such as the S&P 500. When the line rises, the stock is doing better than the market, even if both are falling. It says nothing about dividends or 52-week lows.")
e("q36", "In a stock table, the 'Open' price", "The opening price is the price of the first trade when the market opens that day. The close is the last trade of the day, and the high and low are the day's extremes. The SEC never sets stock prices; supply and demand do.")
e("q37", "You invest $1,000 at 5% interest", "With annual compounding, interest earns interest. Year 1: $1,000 x 1.05 = $1,050. Year 2: $1,050 x 1.05 = $1,102.50. That equals 1,000 x 1.05^2. Simple interest would pay $50 each year, for $1,100, which is why $1,102.50 is slightly higher.")
e("q38", "Marcus deposits $2,000", "For one year, future value = principal + interest. Interest = $2,000 x 0.06 = $120, so FV = $2,000 + $120 = $2,120. With only one period, simple and compound interest give the same answer.")
e("q39", "How much must you invest today at 10%", "This is a present value problem: PV = FV / (1 + i)^n. Two years at 10% means 1.10 x 1.10 = 1.21, so PV = $12,100 / 1.21 = $10,000. Check it forward: $10,000 grows to $11,000 after year 1 and $12,100 after year 2.")
e("q40", "In the formula FV = PV", "In FV = PV x (1 + i)^n, n is the number of compounding periods, often years, and i is the interest rate per period. More periods means more compounding and a larger future value, which is why starting early matters.")
e("q41", "The time value of money means that", "A dollar today is worth more than a dollar later because today's dollar can be invested to earn interest, and because inflation and risk reduce what future dollars are worth. That's why lenders charge interest and why a lump sum now can beat a larger amount later.")
e("q42", "Kai saves part of each paycheck", "Money is a store of value when people can hold it now and use its buying power later. Kai is setting money aside for next summer. Medium of exchange is using money to buy things; unit of measure is using it to price and compare things.")
e("q43", "A customer taps her phone", "Contactless purchasing uses near-field communication (NFC): a phone's digital wallet or a tap-enabled card sends payment information wirelessly to the reader. No cash, check or swipe is needed. A cash advance is borrowing cash against a credit card.")
e("q44", "A currency that is decentralized", "Cryptocurrencies like bitcoin run on decentralized blockchain networks that no government or central bank issues or controls. Fiat money, like the U.S. dollar, is the opposite: government-issued and valuable because the government declares it legal tender.")
e("q45", "Which of the following is a purpose of credit", "Credit lets buyers get goods and services now and pay later, which makes large purchases like homes, cars and education possible. It usually costs interest, so it doesn't eliminate borrowing costs, guarantee lower prices or replace income needed to repay.")
e("q46", "A bank evaluates whether a borrower", "Capacity is the borrower's ability to repay from current income, judged by income, job stability and debt-to-income ratio. Character is trustworthiness and credit history, collateral is property pledged against the loan, and conditions are the loan's purpose and the economy.")
e("q47", "Which is a variable expense", "Variable expenses change from month to month depending on choices and use, and groceries vary with what you buy. Rent, insurance premiums and fixed loan payments are fixed expenses: the same amount every period, which makes them easier to plan.")
e("q48", "Which type of life insurance covers", "Term life insurance covers you for a set term, such as 20 years, and pays only if you die during that time. It builds no savings, which is why it's much cheaper. Whole, universal and variable life are permanent policies with a cash value component.")
e("q49", "Wages, salaries and tips", "Earned income is money you receive for work you perform: wages, salaries, tips, commissions and self-employment income. Passive income comes from things like rental property, and dividends and capital gains are investment income.")
e("q50", "Which type of accounting prepares reports", "Managerial accounting produces internal reports, such as budgets, cost analyses and product profitability, to help managers plan and decide. It isn't required to follow GAAP. Financial accounting reports to outside users like investors and lenders.")
e("q51", "Financial accounting ___ historical data", "Financial accounting reports past results to outside users such as investors, lenders and regulators, following GAAP. Managerial accounting looks forward, using cost and performance data to help managers inside the company plan and decide. (Managerial accountants both record and interpret data; the key difference is the past-versus-future focus and who the reports are for.)",
  q="Financial accounting mainly reports ___ results to outside users, while managerial accounting mainly supports ___ decisions by internal managers.",
  opts=["past; future", "future; past", "estimated; audited", "tax-only; marketing-only"])
e("q52", "A company spent $20,000 on market research", "A sunk cost has already been spent and can't be recovered whatever the company decides. The $20,000 is gone whether or not the product launches, so the decision should be based only on future costs and benefits. Letting it drive the decision is the sunk-cost trap.")
e("q53", "Option A costs $6,000", "Differential (incremental) cost is the difference in cost between two alternatives: $8,500 - $6,000 = $2,500. It's the extra amount you'd pay for choosing B, which is exactly what the decision should weigh against B's extra benefits.")
e("q54", "Which of the following is a direct cost", "A direct cost can be traced to a specific product. The wood in a table goes straight into that table. Factory electricity, the CEO's salary and building insurance are shared by everything the company makes, so they're indirect costs (overhead) that must be allocated.")
e("q55", "Electricity for an entire factory", "Electricity powers the whole factory, so there's no easy way to say how much each of the five products used. That makes it an indirect cost. Indirect costs are assigned to products with an allocation base such as machine hours or square footage.")
e("q56", "Assigning a share of the building's rent", "Cost allocation divides a shared, indirect cost among departments or products using a cost driver that reflects use. Here the driver is square footage: a department using 30% of the space gets 30% of the rent. It doesn't change total rent, only how it's divided.")
e("q57", "A project manager compares the labor hours", "Variance analysis compares planned (budgeted) amounts with actual results. Budgeted versus actual labor hours is a labor efficiency comparison, and the gap tells the manager whether the work took longer than expected and why.")
e("q58", "Variance analysis shows a project needed", "The purpose of variance analysis is to learn from the gap and improve. If a project needed far more labor than estimated, the estimate was probably too low, so the manager should revise time and labor estimates for future projects. Ignoring it or firing the team doesn't fix the planning problem.")
e("q59", "A bakery owner considers whether staying open", "Marginal analysis compares the extra (marginal) revenue of one more unit of activity, here one extra hour, with its extra (marginal) cost, here wages and utilities. If marginal revenue is at least marginal cost, staying open adds profit.")
e("q60", "Cost accounting helps a business", "Cost accounting tracks the full cost of producing goods or services, including materials, labor and overhead, and compares it with standards. That shows where and why costs exceed plan, such as waste or price increases, so managers can improve efficiency. Tax returns, customer acquisition and stock sales are other functions.")
